{"id":"057ab34b-dc9d-46ba-b449-a085ed38200b","object":"chat.completion","created":1784817075,"model":"deepseek-v4-flash","choices":[{"index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"{\n  \"implementation\": \"def solve(case):\\n    import collections\\n    \\n    # Extract payload\\n    if not isinstance(case, dict) or 'payload' not in case:\\n        raise ValueError('Invalid case: missing payload')\\n    payload = case['payload']\\n    if 'nodes' not in payload or 'required_symbols' not in payload:\\n        raise ValueError('Invalid payload: missing nodes or required_symbols')\\n    nodes = payload['nodes']\\n    required_symbols = payload['required_symbols']\\n    \\n    # Validate nodes and build index\\n    if not isinstance(nodes, list):\\n        raise ValueError('nodes must be a list')\\n    node_by_id = {}\\n    for node in nodes:\\n        if not isinstance(node, dict):\\n            raise ValueError('each node must be a dict')\\n        required_fields = ['id', 'parent', 'symbol', 'depth', 'removable', 'utility']\\n        for f in required_fields:\\n            if f not in node:\\n                raise ValueError(f'node missing field {f}')\\n        nid = node['id']\\n        if not isinstance(nid, str):\\n            raise ValueError('node id must be a string')\\n        if nid in node_by_id:\\n            raise ValueError(f'duplicate node id: {nid}')\\n        node_by_id[nid] = node\\n    \\n    # Find root (parent null)\\n    roots = [n for n in nodes if n['parent'] is None]\\n    if len(roots) != 1:\\n        raise ValueError('must have exactly one root node')\\n    root = roots[0]\\n    \\n    # Validate parent references and build children mapping\\n    children = collections.defaultdict(list)\\n    for node in nodes:\\n        pid = node['parent']\\n        if pid is not None:\\n            if pid not in node_by_id:\\n                raise ValueError(f'parent {pid} not found for node {node[\\\"id\\\"]}')\\n            children[pid].append(node['id'])\\n    \\n    # Validate depths (parent depth+1)\\n    from collections import deque\\n    queue = deque([root['id']])\\n    visited = set()\\n    while queue:\\n        nid = queue.popleft()\\n        if nid in visited:\\n            raise ValueError('cycle detected')\\n        visited.add(nid)\\n        node = node_by_id[nid]\\n        for child_id in children[nid]:\\n            child = node_by_id[child_id]\\n            if child['depth'] != node['depth'] + 1:\\n                raise ValueError(f'depth mismatch for {child_id}')\\n            queue.append(child_id)\\n    if len(visited) != len(nodes):\\n        raise ValueError('disconnected nodes')\\n    \\n    # Validate required_symbols: all must exist at least once in the tree\\n    symbol_set = set(node['symbol'] for node in nodes)\\n    for sym in required_symbols:\\n        if sym not in symbol_set:\\n            raise ValueError(f'required symbol {sym} not present in any node')\\n    \\n    # Initialize symbol counts\\n    symbol_counts = collections.Counter()\\n    for node in nodes:\\n        symbol_counts[node['symbol']] += 1\\n    \\n    # Prepare processing order: deeper first, then by node id (ascending)\\n    # We need to process nodes that are removable and not root\\n    process_nodes = [node for node in nodes if node['id'] != root['id'] and node['removable'] == True]\\n    # Sort by (-depth, id)\\n    process_nodes.sort(key=lambda n: (-n['depth'], n['id']))\\n    \\n    removed = set()\\n    removal_order = []\\n    \\n    for node in process_nodes:\\n        nid = node['id']\\n        if nid in removed:\\n            continue\\n        # Check that all children are already removed (or have no children)\\n        child_ids = children.get(nid, [])\\n        if any(cid not in removed for cid in child_ids):\\n            continue\\n        # Tentatively remove\\n        removed.add(nid)\\n        symbol_counts[node['symbol']] -= 1\\n        # Check if all required symbols still present\\n        all_present = all(symbol_counts[sym] > 0 for sym in required_symbols)\\n        if all_present:\\n            removal_order.append(nid)\\n        else:\\n            # Revert\\n            removed.remove(nid)\\n            symbol_counts[node['symbol']] += 1\\n    \\n    retained_ids = [node['id'] for node in nodes if node['id'] not in removed]\\n    retained_ids.sort()\\n    return {'retained': retained_ids, 'removal_order': removal_order}\"\n}","reasoning_content":"We need to write Python code for `solve(case)` that implements grammar-guided node reduction. The task is to reduce a parse tree (given as nodes with parent references, depth, symbol, removable, utility) while preserving required symbols and grammar validity? The description mentions \"grammar-valid deletion and replacement candidates\" but we don't have a grammar. It says \"Perses syntax-guided reduction on a miniature grammar; parser generation and real compiler execution are excluded.\" So likely we need to simulate a reduction algorithm, but without actual grammar. The fixture payload includes nodes with parent, symbol, depth, removable, utility. Required symbols are listed. We need to decide which nodes to remove (tentatively) if they are removable and not required, and if all required symbols are still represented after removal. Also need to consider deeper nodes before shallower nodes (post-order traversal). Use node ID as tie-break at equal depth. Return sorted retained IDs and actual removal order. Also presumably we need to ensure that after removals, the tree remains \"grammar valid\" — but we don't have grammar rules. Perhaps the \"grammar valid\" condition is simply that the tree structure remains a tree (no orphaned children? Actually removal of a node: if we remove a node, its children become children of its parent? In Perses, reduction often involves replacing a node with its children or deleting a subtree. The description says \"Tentatively remove a node only if all required symbols remain represented.\" It doesn't specify what happens to children. Likely we are to remove subtrees (i.e., delete the node and all its descendants), but we must ensure required symbols are still present elsewhere. The \"removable\" flag indicates if the node can be removed. The root and nodes with required symbols are not removable (removable false). Also \"Never remove ... a node with retained children\" — that suggests if we decide to keep a child, we cannot remove parent? Actually \"node with retained children\" means if any child is retained (not removed), then the parent cannot be removed. So we can only remove a node if all its children are also removed? Or if it has no children? In tree reduction, typically you can delete a subtree entirely. But here the condition \"node with retained children\" indicates that if we plan to keep some children, we cannot delete the parent. So essentially we are selecting a set of nodes to delete, and the deletion must be such that the remaining nodes form a connected subtree? Actually if you delete a parent and keep its child, that child becomes orphan. So to maintain tree structure, either we delete the whole subtree or keep it. So the algorithm: we traverse nodes in post-order (deeper first), for each removable node that is not required, we consider tentatively removing it (i.e., marking it for deletion). But we must check that after removal, all required symbols are still present in the remaining tree. Also we must ensure that we don't remove a node that has any child that we are not also removing (since \"node with retained children\" cannot be removed). So before removing a node, all its children must also be removed. That suggests a bottom-up approach: we decide to remove a node only if we have already decided to remove all its descendants (or it has no children). This is typical for Perses: delete subtrees that are redundant. So the algorithm: iterate nodes sorted by depth descending (deeper first), then by node ID as tie-breaker. For each node, if it is removable and not required, and if it has no children that are retained (i.e., all children are also marked for removal or have been removed), then tentatively mark it as removed. Before finalizing, we need to check that all required symbols are still represented in the overall tree (including nodes not removed and also ancestors? Required symbols are just symbols that must appear at least once in the remaining tree). If after marking this node as removed, the total set of symbols (from nodes that are not removed) still contains all required symbols, then we can proceed with removal. Otherwise, we revert. That seems plausible.\n\nAdditionally, we need to return the retained node IDs (sorted? \"Return sorted retained IDs and the actual removal order.\" Probably retain order is sorted ascending by ID? Possibly. Removal order is the order in which nodes were removed (the sequence of removals). We'll output a dict with \"retained\" (list of IDs sorted) and \"removal_order\" (list of IDs in order they were removed). Also we need to validate fields: check that nodes have required fields, that root exists, that parent references are valid, etc. If invalid, raise ValueError.\n\nWe'll implement a function `solve(case)` that takes a dict with \"nodes\" and \"required_symbols\". Build a dict mapping id to node. Build children mapping. Then post-order traversal using depth and ID. Perform the reduction as described. We'll maintain a set of removed nodes. For each node in traversal order, check if it can be removed: (1) removable flag True; (2) not in required symbols (but note: required symbols are symbols, not node IDs. A node might have a required symbol but the required list is per symbol, not per node. However, we cannot remove a node that has a required symbol if that symbol appears only there? Actually condition: \"Never remove the root, a required symbol, or a node with retained children.\" \"Required symbol\" likely means a node whose symbol is in required_symbols. But if multiple nodes have same symbol, we can remove one as long as at least one remains. The condition \"Tentatively remove a node only if all required symbols remain represented.\" So we check after removal that the set of symbols in remaining nodes covers all required symbols. So we don't forbid removing a node that has a required symbol as long as that symbol is also present elsewhere. But the condition \"Never remove ... a required symbol\" suggests that nodes whose symbol is in required_symbols cannot be removed at all. The fixture data: required_symbols: [\"root\",\"s1\"]. In nodes, root has symbol \"root\", n1 has symbol \"s1\", n4 also has symbol \"s1\". So root is not removable anyway. n1 is removable? In fixture, n1 has removable false. So it's fine. But n4 is removable true and has symbol \"s1\". If we remove n4, s1 still appears in n1, so required symbols still represented. But is that allowed? The instruction says \"Never remove the root, a required symbol\" — that seems to say any node whose symbol is required cannot be removed. But then \"Tentatively remove a node only if all required symbols remain represented\" would be redundant for those nodes. Let's read carefully: \"Never remove the root, a required symbol, or a node with retained children.\" I think \"required symbol\" means a node that is marked as required (like in the node's properties). But there is no such property. Alternatively, it might mean a node whose symbol is in required_symbols. However, the second condition \"Tentatively remove a node only if all required symbols remain represented\" suggests that a node with a required symbol can be removed if that symbol is still present elsewhere. So there is ambiguity. To be safe, I'll interpret \"Never remove ... a required symbol\" as not removing a node that is the only occurrence of that required symbol? But that would be covered by the \"all required symbols remain represented\" check. I think the intended meaning is that nodes that are specifically marked as \"required\" (like the root) cannot be removed. In the fixture, only root has removable=false and parent null. Others have removable flags. But \"required symbol\" might be a separate concept. The required_symbols list indicates symbols that must survive the reduction. So nodes that have those symbols are not necessarily individually protected. For example, if there are two nodes with symbol \"s1\", one can be removed. The instruction \"Never remove ... a required symbol\" might be a misstatement or means \"never remove a node if its symbol is required and it's the only one\"? But I'll follow the more logical approach: we never remove nodes that are marked as not removable (removable false). That includes root and possibly others. Then we also never remove a node if it would cause a required symbol to disappear. So the algorithm: for each node in post-order, if removable and not required (i.e., removable==True) and all its children are already removed (or have no children), then we tentatively remove it. Then check if all required symbols are still present in the remaining nodes (including those not removed). If yes, finalize removal. If no, undo tentative removal. That matches typical Perses reduction.\n\nWe'll implement that. Return `{\"retained\": sorted(list_of_retained_ids), \"removal_order\": list_of_removed_ids_in_order}`. Need to ensure retention order sorted ascending (since IDs are strings like \"root\",\"n1\",... but sorting lexicographically might not be numeric. However, IDs are \"root\",\"n1\",\"n2\",... so lexical sort will put \"n1\",\"n10\"? There's no n10 in fixture, but to be safe, we can sort by the order they appear? Or by integer extraction? The spec says \"Return sorted retained IDs\" — likely by the natural order of IDs as given in input? Possibly alphabetically. We'll sort by the string ID. That should be fine.\n\nAlso validate: check that each node has required fields: id, parent, symbol, depth, removable, utility. Also check that the tree is valid (root has parent null, all children have valid parent, no cycles). If invalid, raise ValueError.\n\nWe'll also handle the case where required_symbols might include symbols not present? Probably not, but we can check.\n\nNow write the Python code as a string in JSON.\n\nNote: The code must be self-contained, no imports beyond standard library. We'll use collections.defaultdict for children mapping.\n\nWe'll define `solve(case)` inside the implementation. The function should return a JSON-serializable object.\n\nImplementation plan:\n\n1. Extract `nodes` and `required_symbols` from `case` (which is a dict with \"payload\" containing those). Actually case is the full case dict? In the fixture, each case has \"payload\" containing nodes and required_symbols. So `solve(case)` receives the case dict, we need to access case[\"payload\"][\"nodes\"] and case[\"payload\"][\"required_symbols\"]. The description says \"Return one JSON object with exactly one key, `implementation`. Its value must be Python source defining `solve(case)`. The function must return a JSON-serializable object, must not mutate `case`.\" So we receive the entire case object, not just payload. We'll parse accordingly.\n\n2. Validate:\n   - case is dict, has \"payload\" key, payload has \"nodes\" and \"required_symbols\".\n   - nodes is a list of dicts.\n   - required_symbols is a list of strings.\n   - Each node has the required fields.\n   - Build node_dict by id.\n   - Check that root exists (id \"root\"? Not necessarily, but we can check parent null node). The root is the node with parent null. There should be exactly one.\n   - Check that all parent references point to existing nodes.\n   - Check that depths are consistent? Optional but we can verify if depth matches parent depth+1. If not, raise ValueError.\n\n3. Build children mapping: dict of parent_id -> list of child ids.\n\n4. Create a set of removed_ids = set().\n\n5. Create a list removal_order = [].\n\n6. Determine the order of nodes to process: sort nodes by (depth, id) descending depth first, then ascending id? Actually \"deeper before shallower\" means descending depth. For tie at same depth, use node ID as stable tie break. We'll sort by (-depth, id). For same depth, id ascending gives deterministic order.\n\n7. For each node in that order:\n   - If node[\"removable\"] is False, skip.\n   - If node[\"id\"] in removed_ids? Actually we only consider nodes not already removed.\n   - Check if all children of this node are either not present (no children) or are in removed_ids. That is, if any child not in removed_ids, we cannot remove this node because it has retained children.\n   - Tentatively mark node as removed: add to removed_ids.\n   - Now compute the set of symbols present among all nodes not in removed_ids. We need to check if every symbol in required_symbols is present.\n   - We can precompute a symbol count map. Efficient: maintain a Counter of symbols for nodes not removed. Initially count all nodes. Then when we tentatively remove a node, decrement its symbol count. If after decrement, all required symbols have count >= 1, then finalize removal. Otherwise revert: remove node from removed_ids and do not add to removal_order.\n   - If finalize, append node[\"id\"] to removal_order.\n\n8. After processing all nodes, retained_ids = [node[\"id\"] for node in nodes if node[\"id\"] not in removed_ids]. Sort retained_ids (string sort). Return {\"retained\": retained_ids, \"removal_order\": removal_order}.\n\nEdge cases: If a required symbol is not present initially, raise ValueError. Also if after removals a required symbol is missing, but we check before each removal, so only possible if initial state missing. Also if root node has removable flag True? But fixture shows root removable false. But we should enforce root not removable.\n\nWe'll also ensure we don't remove root even if removable flag is True? But spec says \"Never remove the root\". So we should skip root regardless of removable flag. We can check if node is root (parent null) and skip.\n\nNow write the code.\n\nPotential pitfalls: The \"required symbol\" condition might be interpreted differently. But given the constraints, we'll go with the above.\n\nWe'll include docstring and comments for clarity.\n\nLet's produce the JSON with the implementation string. Ensure proper escaping.\n\nI'll write the Python code now."},"logprobs":null,"finish_reason":"stop"}],"usage":{"prompt_tokens":1852,"completion_tokens":4059,"total_tokens":5911,"prompt_tokens_details":{"cached_tokens":1536},"completion_tokens_details":{"reasoning_tokens":2965},"prompt_cache_hit_tokens":1536,"prompt_cache_miss_tokens":316},"system_fingerprint":"fp_8b330d02d0_prod0820_fp8_kvcache_20260402"}